{#-
Extractors:
  - response: AsIsExtractor()

A branch of a sphere (a schism, denomination, school or sub-order): its
core, in sphere-core's flat shape and wording, written against its
parent's labelled items. The reply drops inherited items by label
("drops") and an item may replace inherited ones ("overrides"), as
create-thing's inherited facts are overridden by label. No probe has run
this prompt yet.

`parent_name`, `parent_summary` and `parent_timeline` ("<year>: <text>")
are the parent's; `inherited` is `[(label, drawer title, line)]`, its
items as the branch inherits them. `drawers` is `[(type, title)]`, the
parent's writable drawers, and `types` `[(type, help)]` the drawer types
the reply may write. `named` is sphere-core's.
-#}
<|SECTION:TASK|>
You create a branch of {{ parent_name }}, a {{ label }} in a fantasy world, from the owner's short prompt: a schism, denomination, school or sub-order that splits from it now. Treat the prompt as intent, not a spec: invent plausible detail generously to fill it out. Keep everything the owner wrote.

The branch inherits everything {{ parent_name }} holds, except what it drops or replaces. Write only what makes it different: what it rejects, and what it holds instead or in addition.

{% if agent -%}
The branch acts in the world: it decides, rules, declares or teaches, so its timeline is what it has said and done.
{%- else -%}
The branch is a body of knowledge or custom that is acted upon, so its timeline is how it has changed: what began, changed or was banned, proven or disproven.
{%- endif %}

Owner's prompt:
{{ prompt }}

{{ parent_name }}:
{{ parent_summary }}
{%- if parent_timeline %}
Timeline:
{% for line in parent_timeline -%}
- {{ line }}
{% endfor %}
{%- endif %}

What the branch inherits from {{ parent_name }}, each with its label and drawer:
{% for label, title, line in inherited -%}
- {{ label }} ({{ title }}): {{ line }}
{% else -%}
- (nothing yet)
{% endfor %}
{%- if named %}

Named in this prompt (these already exist in the world: use them as they are, and never invent a new one in their place):
{% for written, heading, facts in named -%}
"{{ written }}" means {{ heading }}:
{% for text in facts -%}
- {{ text }}
{% endfor %}
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}

Its drawers, inherited from {{ parent_name }} (type: title):
{% for type, title in drawers -%}
- {{ type }}: {{ title }}
{% else -%}
- (none: pick what it needs)
{% endfor %}
The drawer types:
{% for type, help in types -%}
- {{ type }}: {{ help }}
{% endfor %}
The world's calendar: it is now {{ now_label }}, year {{ now_year }}{% if era %} of the {{ era }}{% endif %}. The months are {{ month_names | join(", ") }}.
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
<|SECTION:FORMAT|>
Reply with only one JSON object, shaped exactly like this example:
{"name": "...", "summary": "Two sentences.", "aka": [{"name": "...", "tag": "common", "everyday": true}], "timeline": [{"year": YEAR, "text": "What happened, in one short sentence."}], "drawers": [{"type": "beliefs", "title": "..."}], "drops": ["F2"], "items": [{"drawer": "the drawer's title", "name": "...", "text": "One short sentence.", "kind": "lore", "overrides": ["F5"]}, {"drawer": "the drawer's title", "text": "A plain statement, in one short sentence."}], "names": [{"name": "...", "kind": "place", "aka": []}]}

- "name": the branch's name. Invent one if the prompt doesn't give it.
- "summary": two sentences: what it is, how it split from {{ parent_name }}, and what it stands for.
- "timeline": 1 to 3 moments in its own history, oldest first, each {"year", "text"}: something that happened in that year, such as the split. "year" is a year of this calendar, as a bare number no later than {{ now_year }}; "YEAR" in the example stands for that number.
- "drawers": only drawers the branch needs that {{ parent_name }} lacks; it keeps every drawer above. "type" is one of: {{ types | map(attribute=0) | join(", ") }}. An empty list is fine.
- "drops": the labels of the inherited items the branch rejects outright. An empty list is fine.
- "items": what the branch holds that {{ parent_name }} doesn't: 1 to 3 items for each drawer it differs in, each naming its drawer by its title in "drawer". Never list an inherited item again.
- Each item is {"drawer", "name", "text", "kind"}. When the item is a thing of its own that could get its own entry (a rite, an office, a council, a doctrine, a text, a site), "name" is its proper name, such as "the Dawn Vigil", and "kind" is lore, place (a site), item (an object) or event. For a plain statement ("Priests may not marry."), give only "drawer" and "text". "text" is one short sentence, under 20 words.
- "overrides" on an item: the labels of the inherited items it replaces. Leave it out when it replaces none.
- "aka": other names for the branch itself, if it has any, each {"name", "tag", "everyday"}. "tag" is "proper" for another formal name, or "common" for what people in the world actually call it day to day. Set "everyday" true on the one common name people use most, false on the rest. An empty list is fine.
- "names": after the rest, every proper name your summary, timeline and items use, other than the branch's own and its items' own names: people, places, peoples and species, named events, named groups and named objects, new or already in the world. Each is {"name", "kind", "aka"}: "name" in full, as you wrote it; "kind" one of character, place, species, race, sphere, event, item, lore; "aka" any other names you used for the same thing. A faith or church, an order, academy, guild or council, a culture, or a field of knowledge or magic is "sphere", with a "template": religion, order, culture or field, only these (a country or realm is a place; a rank or office is lore). Proper names only: never a description such as "the postmaster" or "a war", nor a title on its own such as "the Tsar", nor a people's adjective such as "Moscavian" on its own. An empty list is fine.

Keep every text to one sentence. No commentary, no markdown fences, just the JSON object.
<|CLOSE_SECTION|>
